Is the data linearly separable?
    Yes → try Logistic Regression or SVM (linear kernel) first

Do you need to explain the model to non-technical stakeholders?
    Yes → Decision Tree or Logistic Regression (interpretable)

Do you just want the highest accuracy on tabular data?
    → Random Forest or Gradient Boosting

Is the dataset very small (< 200 rows)?
    → KNN, Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes (simple models)

Is training speed critical?
    → Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes (fastest to train)
====
Yes — Logistic Regression works here too
Despite the name, Logistic Regression is a classification algorithm. It estimates the probability of each class

from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV

model = LogisticRegression(random_state=42, max_iter=1000)

param_grid = {
    'C':       [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100],  # regularisation strength
    'solver':  ['lbfgs', 'saga'],         # optimisation algorithm
    'penalty': ['l2', 'l1'],              # type of regularisation
}

grid_search = GridSearchCV(model, param_grid, cv=5, scoring='accuracy')
grid_search.fit(X_train, y_train)
===
C is the key hyperparameter — it is the inverse of regularisation strength:
Small C (e.g. 0.01) → strong regularisation → simpler boundary → may underfit
Large C (e.g. 100)  → weak regularisation  → complex boundary → may overfit
